yagr's default x time formatter: for a 24h range (rangeMs <= DAY) → `{HH}:{mm}:{ss}` — hours with seconds, a bit noisy ("04:00:00"). I'd rather format hour ticks as "HH:mm" and include the date when the day changes. Custom `axes.x.values`:

```ts
const tickHourFmt = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('ru-RU', {hour: '2-digit', minute: '2-digit'});
const tickDayFmt = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('ru-RU', {day: 'numeric', month: 'short'});
axes: {
  x: {
    values: (_u, ticks) => {
      let prevDay = -1;
      return ticks.map((t) => {
        const d = new Date(t);
        const day = d.getDate();
        const label = tickHourFmt.format(d);
        if (day !== prevDay) { prevDay = day; return `${tickDayFmt.format(d)}, ${label}`; }  // first tick of a new day gets date — but label length grows
        return label;
      });
    },
  },
}
```

Hmm, that makes one long label ("7 нояб., 00:00") that can collide with neighbors. Alternative: two-line label via `\n` (uPlot supports multiline values with `\n`). So new-day tick → `00:00\n7 нояб`. Nice. But ticks are values received in **uPlot domain**: uPlot x scale units — ms here? `ms: 1` means timestamps in ms... uPlot internally: with `ms: 1`, the values passed to formatters are in ms. yagr's own formatter does `new Date(rawValue / msm)` where msm = timeMultiplier = 1 → treats tick value as ms. So ticks are ms timestamps. My formatter: `new Date(t)` directly. ✓

uPlot values returning multiline strings: supported (uPlot handles "\n" splits for axis values). ✓

Splits for x: uPlot default time splits produce nice hour boundaries. Keep default splits, override only `values`.

Y axis: `values: (_u, ticks) => ticks.map(v => `${v} %`)`. Default splits for 0..100 → 0,20,...,100. ✓ (uPlot numeric splits default.)

